Organize the supplied canonical statements into clear memory facts about the subject. Return the groups and their presentation metadata; prose rendering happens separately.

For each group, describe its specific event, object, relationship, habit or preference in memory_scope. Group repeated support and complementary details of that same thing. Keep different events and independent plans separate. Assign every supplied claim ID to exactly one group. Respect the schema's member limit: when a scope has more evidence than one group can hold, use several complementary groups without discarding evidence.

Use the original statements to decide their scope. Represent conflicting accounts separately, with their own members and attribution. Choose an allowed section. Use state="history" for completed events or ended states, otherwise "current". Preserve the scope established by supplied dates and source times.

Choose prominence="briefing" for responsibilities, commitments, dependencies, decisions, constraints, useful working preferences, unresolved questions, and consequential current context. Choose prominence="detail" for routine observations and general sentiments. Uncertainty does not make a commitment or prerequisite unimportant. Both levels remain searchable and cited.
